## Audit findings — hitches / long-session stability

Read-only audit complete. No files were modified.

### 1. Two authored-encounter systems scan the entire entity list every flight tick — `mothlight.js:252`, `kilnback.js:309`
```
mothlight.js:249  if (this._restoring || state.mode !== 'flight' || state.tick === this._lastTick || ...) return;
mothlight.js:252  for (const e of state.entityList) if (e.alive && e.data?.lootTableId === C.enemyId) this.bind(e);
kilnback.js:306   if (this._restoring || state.mode !== 'flight' || state.tick === this._lastTick || ...) return;
kilnback.js:309   for (const e of state.entityList) if (e.alive && e.data?.lootTableId === C.enemyId && !e.data.kilnbackCaskId) this.bind(e);
```
The guard only stops at `mode !== 'flight'` and per-tick dedupe. Both scan *all* live entities **every flight tick even when the encounter has never spawned in the save** — the exact shape the codebase already replaced elsewhere with a quiet-latch/membership-version scan (`salvageActions.js:300-317`) or typed buckets (`state.entityIndex.ships`, used at `combat.js:1272`, `weapons.js:641`, `countermeasures.js:787`; `countermeasures` scans `index.ships`, not `entityList`). Impact: linear per-tick cost that grows with world population and never resets across a long session. Files are clean (not foreign-lane).

### 2. Per-tick allocation churn in `reactiveMatter.update()` — `reactiveMatter.js:566-577`
```
566  const contacts = [], deposits = [], seen = new Set();
567  for (const row of this.pending.splice(0)) {
573    contacts.push({ ...row, ... });
577  const result = stepReactiveMatter(world, { dt: stepDt, contacts });
```
The early return at `:546` only fires when docked / not flight / paused. While flying, every tick allocates `contacts`, `deposits`, a `new Set()`, **and** a fresh array from `this.pending.splice(0)` even when nothing is pending, plus the options object at `:577` — ~5 allocations/tick (≈300/s) that exist purely as GC pressure in the steady state.

### 3. Per-tick `Object.keys/values` + array-literal allocations across registered systems (aggregate GC pressure)
Each runs unconditionally per flight tick and allocates an array/collection object:
- `surrenderRecovery.js:151` `for (const record of Object.values(own.records))`
- `pirateRumor.js:80` `for (const rec of Object.values(own.zones))`
- `survivorPod.js:680` `for (const rec of Object.values(own.promotedByPoint))`
- `wingMorale.js:214` `for (const entityId of Object.keys(own.scatter))`
- `missions.js:1857` `this._pendingCapitalBossDetaches = new Set()`
- `countermeasures.js:421` `const channels = []` (plus an object + two closures per ready channel)
- `crafting.js:187` `const completedIds = []`
- `environmentMaterials.js:206,209` `const waiting = []` / `state.materialReactions = []`

Individually small; collectively they add a steady allocation rate on the sim thread. Systems that already do this correctly: `traffic.js:5657-5720`, `bountyHunt.js:172-173`, `fields.js:967-968`, `masslineThreats.js:155`, `aiPorts.js:177`, `lawSecurity.js:514` (all reuse scratch buffers).

### 4. Per-frame `Object.entries` allocation in force-language presentation — `emergentPayloadPresentation.js:191`
```
191  for (const [owner, pair] of Object.entries(world.owners || {})) ...
```
Inside per-frame `update()`. The sibling `fieldForcePresentation.js:209` already avoids this with a cached `_mergedList` scratch array, so this is the outlier in the same subsystem.

### Verified clean (no defect)
Loop/tick spine (`loop.js`, `simulationRunner.js`, `presentationRunner.js`, `sim.js`, `eventBus.js`); `traffic.js` heavy update (WeakMap/scratch reuse); `salvageActions` quiet-latch; docking/listener lifecycles (`stationContactLoadBoundary` destroys its subs, `settings.js:1626` `onHide` clears the rebind capture listeners, `watchlistHud`, `bootVisualizer`, `mobile/laneTouchSurface`, `mobile/view`, `presentationAdapters` role-briefing timer, `telemetry` page-lifecycle); `pipelineReadiness.js:653`, `crucible.js:1711`, `stationServices.js:523`, `palletJack`/`redKite`/`ilexArk`/`sableVenn` scans (all gated or binding-cached); render per-frame paths (`renderer.drawPreparedFrame`, vfx, camera) are allocation-free in the steady state.
